92186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1007 "Methods of Proof" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1008 "This course introduces students to proof-writing essentials, bridging the gap between pure problem-solving and constructing formal mathematical arguments. Students will formalize their understanding of logic, sets, and functions while delving into new concepts such as congruence, relations, and cardinality.\nThis course serves as ideal preparation for students wishing to pursue undergraduate studies in formal mathematical disciplines, including Discrete Mathematics, Abstract Algebra, and Real Analysis.\nThe prerequisite for Methods of Proof is single-variable calculus, which would be satisfied by completion of either Calculus II, AP Calculus BC, or Mathematical Foundations III." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1009 "Students begin by mastering the basics of logic and logical operations, including statements, predicates, connectives, logical laws, logical equivalence, conditional and biconditional statements, quantifiers, inference, and formal and informal language. By solidifying their grasp of these logical fundamentals, students will have a secure underpinning that enables them to embark on their journey into proof-writing confidently.\nA robust knowledge of set theory is another cornerstone of mathematical proof-writing. Students will extend and formalize their understanding of sets and learn to translate between logical and set operations. By the end of the course, students will appreciate how set theory provides a comprehensive toolkit for proving mathematical results.\nAfter mastering these fundamentals, students will explore various proof-writing techniques, including direct proof, proof by cases, proof by contrapositive, proof by contradiction, disproof, and mathematical induction. The course emphasizes proving concepts related to parity, divisibility, congruence, sequences, functions, set identities, and relations. Students will have multiple opportunities to test and refine their proof construction and comprehension skills throughout the course and in varied contexts\nThis challenging yet engaging course helps students with strong mathematical foundations build a solid base for further progression into advanced undergraduate courses." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1012 76 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1013 "MOP" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046673 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "53322b4c-7036-5cde-89d2-8e430f49ef2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428783 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428784 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428785 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428786 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428787 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Inequalities Involving Exponential Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1033 2857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1034 1.3937071064146724 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048553 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "a932fb93-8c00-5050-af2c-a8a7f3976265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049055 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049055 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049055 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049055 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049055 #edb/ref 1031 "Infinite Limits of Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049055 #edb/ref 1033 4344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049055 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d62806de-3c81-582d-87a5-c822d0229c05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049595 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049595 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049595 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049595 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049595 #edb/ref 1031 "The Limit of a Null Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049595 #edb/ref 1033 4342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049595 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "0330a624-69eb-5012-8637-ffdcc435c5a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1031 "The Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1033 680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050067 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2a8497f4-0ed3-5e71-a11d-456426f348f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Inequalities Involving Geometric Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1033 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1034 1.1824841801908395 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050598 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5761edb2-80f5-563f-ad9c-bf729c12d152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092020 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092020 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3aac9bd-c462-5664-a196-278a9671fbbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092020 #edb/ref 1074 "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{-2}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{n-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{n-3} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {8}^{n-3} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({8}^{n-3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(8) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092021 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092021 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4af09b88-5f57-572f-ae64-bfe64e94450d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092021 #edb/ref 1074 "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16}  = \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{5-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,000\\,1 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac { \\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092022 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092022 #edb/ref 1071 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54,$ and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "154a5006-6c4f-58c7-a228-ccbefabd5050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092022 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and  $r= 3,$ we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1}\\[3pt]\n\t\ta_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1}\\[3pt]\n\t54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3\\[3pt]\n\t54&=27a_1\\[3pt]\n\ta_1&=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >1\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(3)^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the First Term Exceeding a Given Value" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186092020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092360 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0e3b20b5-9427-538d-9392-aa137b5d8126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092361 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092361 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092361 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092361 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218609