[17592186092398 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092398 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092398 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186092398 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fc499402-7fb4-528a-a437-297d7d142802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093288 #edb/ref 1061 466081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ae31a98-46a1-5e73-a782-739c76a5378c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093289 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093289 #edb/ref 1061 466082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06291b9a-e86c-591b-9600-116684c83888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093290 #edb/ref 1061 466086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d220ad1-000f-5dc5-b5a7-2b4c4b28512e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093291 #edb/ref 1061 466089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093291 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ee758162-fb37-5457-9276-2fe4d3d15bf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093291 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093400 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following geometric sequence: \n\n$$\n3,\\quad 6,\\quad 12,\\quad 24, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose we want to find the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$ We can do this by creating and solving an inequality.\n\nFirst, let's construct a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence. The first term of the sequence is $a_1=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 6 3 = 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $3\\,000\\,000.$ So, we can set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{n-1} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}(2) & >\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}(2)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\,000)} {\\log (2)} + 1 \\approx 20.932.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$\n\nTo check our answer, we can easily verify that $a_{20} < 3\\, 000\\, 000$ and $a_{21} > 3\\, 000\\, 000\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{20} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{20-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{19}=1\\,572\\,864<3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {a}_{21} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{21-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{20}=3\\,145\\,728>3\\,000\\,000\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $a_{21}$ is indeed the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093400 #edb/ref 1065 2886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093400 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9a55e4c5-cd46-5a39-97aa-f29fe4e37185" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093400 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293779 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293779 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293779 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dab19498-0f30-5fbf-b741-364f1b7f05f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294237 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186293779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294237 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294237 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294237 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186293779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294237 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "56b7f67d-5586-52c4-a88d-588629b42516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294937 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186294237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294937 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294937 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294937 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,8,\\,32,\\,128,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294937 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29e6ffa8-9f33-56f5-810e-d7c6edc1eff6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186294937 #edb/ref 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $58\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >58\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >58\\,000 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >29\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈8.412.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $58\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324275 #edb/ref 1080 "the $42$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324275 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324275 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24eebd01-d696-5583-866b-bc7ee0975ae6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324765 #edb/ref 1080 "the $39$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324765 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324765 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5efa3e05-6fbd-561f-aad6-9039ff65a874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325115 #edb/ref 1080 "the $38$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325115 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325115 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "869369c2-c60a-5bfe-9e90-d7fc384dd4c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325979 #edb/ref 1080 "the $41$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325979 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325979 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e425578a-3944-5da8-bbf6-a67efada93ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326012 #edb/ref 1080 "the $40$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326012 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326012 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e7c67aa3-aae6-50c4-9e30-cdb98ed82129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186326012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186326012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326264 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1793d83a-a4d4-5328-b990-9f3e282f524a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326772 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326772 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326772 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326772 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326772 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5d1d92f-ad9b-5aee-bc72-594097ea04d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326772 #edb/ref 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333447 #edb/ref 1080 "the $10$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333447 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333447 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31f79c15-e015-58ee-9759-1651faf25e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333527 #edb/ref 1080 "the $12$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333527 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417