6335932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5456760-9cdb-54e3-82ce-b6d6e704f8ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335932 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=3$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 3 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{3}^{4}} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{-4}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{n-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $907.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n-5} & >907 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(3)}^{n-5} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ (n-5)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(907) \\\\ n-5 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(907)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+5≈11.199\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $907.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357841 #edb/ref 1080 "the $9$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357841 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357841 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a68a45c-7e16-5fb2-afee-3dfbb5fdb55b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358320 #edb/ref 1080 "the $7$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358320 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358320 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42a3c94b-95c3-5072-8e13-4256db80a58f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359160 #edb/ref 1080 "the $10$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a1a780c1-259a-5ea0-bd30-a23c589220b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359530 #edb/ref 1080 "the $6$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "caa963c4-4072-5bbe-b660-3ffaf8cff6bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359928 #edb/ref 1080 "the $8$th term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359928 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f588cff2-7042-54f7-9627-96d8e9d8f0ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186357841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360408 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e90aa030-babb-5358-a2e1-4ec64e1f8392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360806 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360806 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360806 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360806 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n4,\\,28,\\,196,\\,1\\,372,\\,…\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360806 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95917747-a74b-509e-9924-fa8c1433f09e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360806 #edb/ref 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{28}{4}=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {7}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 4\\cdot {7}^{n-1} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {7}^{n-1} & >500\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({7}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(7) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)}+1≈7.744\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
